---
// Is my donation eligible for Gift Aid? Linked from the Gift Aid tick-box on
// /donate and the committee's tablet (declaration.ts ELIGIBILITY_PATH), so a donor
// confirming "my donation is eligible" can read what that means.
//
// The common reasons a gift does not qualify come first, in plain words; the
// detail points at GOV.UK rather than restating it, so HMRC's rules are read from
// HMRC and this page cannot drift out of date with them. Hand-written rather than
// Keystatic for the same reason the declaration is config: it is what a donor
// confirms by ticking the box.
//
// Prerendered: nothing here reads runtime config.
export const prerender = true;

import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { CHARITY_NAME, CHARITY_NUMBER, CONTACT_EMAIL, UPDATE_NOTICE } from '../lib/gift-aid/declaration.ts';

const GOV = {
  donor: 'https://www.gov.uk/donating-to-charity/gift-aid',
  whatQualifies: 'https://www.gov.uk/claim-gift-aid/what-you-can-claim-it-on',
  rules: 'https://www.gov.uk/guidance/gift-aid-what-donations-charities-and-cascs-can-claim-on',
  incomeTax: 'https://www.gov.uk/income-tax',
  cgt: 'https://www.gov.uk/capital-gains-tax',
};
---

<Layout
  title="Is my donation eligible for Gift Aid?"
  description="The common reasons a donation to the Dartington Recreation Association can't have Gift Aid added, with links to HMRC's full rules on GOV.UK."
>
  <section class="mw-zone-hero" style="padding-bottom: var(--space-7);">
    <div class="mw-wrap">
      <span class="mw-eyebrow">Gift Aid</span>
      <h1>Is my donation eligible?</h1>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mw-sect" style="padding-top: 0;">
    <div class="mw-wrap">
      <article class="mw-article">
        <p class="lead">
          Gift Aid lets us claim an extra 25p from HMRC for every £1 you give, at no cost to you.
          Most donations qualify. These are the usual reasons one doesn't:
        </p>

        <ul class="ga-reasons">
          <li>
            <strong>You don't pay enough UK tax.</strong>
            In the tax year you give, you need to pay at least as much Income Tax or Capital Gains
            Tax as all the charities you Gift Aid to will claim: 25p for every £1.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>It isn't your own money.</strong>
            For example, money you collected from other people, a business or company's money, or
            a donation you're making on someone else's behalf.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>You're paying for something.</strong>
            Swimming sessions, room hire, event tickets, raffle tickets, and food or drink are
            purchases, not donations.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>You're getting something valuable in return.</strong>
            A gift or benefit worth more than HMRC's limits stops the donation qualifying.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          If none of these apply, you can tick the Gift Aid box. If you're not sure, leave it
          unticked: your donation is just as welcome without it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="tax">Paying enough tax</h2>
        <p>
          Gift Aid is tax you've already paid, given to the charity. So it only works if you paid
          enough <a href={GOV.incomeTax}>Income Tax</a> or <a href={GOV.cgt}>Capital Gains Tax</a> in
          that tax year (6 April to 5 April) to cover what every charity you've given to claims. If
          you didn't, HMRC can ask you to pay the difference. VAT and Council Tax don't count.
        </p>
        <p>
          GOV.UK explains this for donors: <a href={GOV.donor}>Donating to charity: Gift Aid</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="own-money">Your own money</h2>
        <p>
          The donation has to come from you personally. Donations from limited companies can't
          have Gift Aid added, and neither can gifts made on behalf of other people or a group.
          Each person giving can make their own declaration instead.
        </p>

        <h2 id="payments">Payments, tickets and benefits</h2>
        <p>
          Paying for goods or services isn't a donation, even when the money goes to the charity
          and even if it's described as "voluntary". At Meadowbrook that includes pool sessions,
          room bookings, event and Extravaganza tickets, and raffle tickets. If you pay more than
          the asking price, the extra on top can be a donation.
        </p>
        <p>
          A donation still qualifies if you receive a small thank-you, but not if what you receive
          is worth more than HMRC's limits. HMRC sets out the benefit rule, events, auctions,
          membership fees and sponsored challenges in
          <a href={GOV.rules}>Gift Aid: what donations charities can claim on</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="other">Other donations that don't qualify</h2>
        <p>
          Donations through Payroll Giving, charity vouchers or cards (such as CAF vouchers) and
          gifts of shares can't have Gift Aid added through us. The full list is on
          <a href={GOV.whatQualifies}>What you can claim Gift Aid on</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="changes">If things change</h2>
        <p>
          {UPDATE_NOTICE} Email us at <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
        </p>

        <p class="ga-charity">
          {CHARITY_NAME}, registered charity number {CHARITY_NUMBER}.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .ga-reasons {
    list-style: none;
    padding: var(--space-5);
    margin: var(--space-5) 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    background: #f4f8ef;
    border: 1.5px solid #a8d18c;
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
  }
  .ga-reasons li { line-height: 1.55; }
  .ga-reasons strong { display: block; }
  .ga-charity {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
    font-size: 0.875em;
    opacity: 0.75;
  }
</style>
